</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Age</strong></td>
<td>35–55</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Occupation</strong></td>
<td>Small business owner, government employee, self-employed</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Location</strong></td>
<td>Across Guwahati</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Income</strong></td>
<td>₹40,000–₹1,20,000/month</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Current Behavior</strong></td>
<td>Has a &quot;regular&quot; store he visits. Dislikes online shopping because &quot;you can&#39;t feel the fabric&quot; and &quot;sizes are always wrong.&quot; Occasionally his children order clothes for him from Myntra — he&#39;s been disappointed 50% of the time.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Why Anil uses ThreadIt:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>He&#39;s ordering from the SAME stores he already visits — this removes the trust barrier</li>
<li>He can see the actual product listed by his known store, not some generic marketplace photo</li>
<li>If we can get his kids to show him the app, he&#39;ll convert</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>What could stop Anil:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Onboarding friction — if the app requires too many steps, he&#39;s gone</li>
<li>If he can&#39;t find &quot;his&quot; store on the app — he doesn&#39;t want to discover new stores, he wants his regular one</li>
<li>Payment — he may prefer COD (Cash on Delivery) initially</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Anil&#39;s activation trigger:</strong> His son says &quot;Papa, your regular store is on this app, you can order from home.&quot;</p>
<h2>Customer Adoption Curve</h2>
<pre><code>Innovators (Month 1-2):     Rohit (last-minute) + Priya (college trendsetter)
Early Adopters (Month 2-4): Arjun (time-starved professional) + Meera (busy parent)
Early Majority (Month 4-8): Anil (reluctant online shopper) + general adoption
</code></pre>
<p><strong>This is important for GTM:</strong> We don&#39;t market to everyone at once. We start with Rohit and Priya&#39;s use cases, build order volume, then expand messaging.</p>
<h2>Anti-Personas — Who We Should NOT Target</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Anti-Persona</th>
<th>Why Not</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>The Ultra-Price-Sensitive Shopper</strong></td>
<td>Will compare ThreadIt prices to Meesho wholesale prices. We will always lose on price. Don&#39;t acquire them — their LTV is negative after accounting for support costs and returns.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>The Fashion Enthusiast Who Wants 10,000 Options</strong></td>
<td>Wants Myntra&#39;s infinite scroll. Our catalog will always be smaller — limited by what local stores carry. We can&#39;t compete on selection breadth.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>The Luxury Buyer</strong></td>
<td>Buying a ₹15,000 outfit is a high-involvement decision. They want the in-store experience — mirrors, trial rooms, personal attention. We can&#39;t replicate that.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>The Recreational Browser</strong></td>
<td>Opens apps to browse with no intent to buy. Will hurt our conversion metrics without contributing to revenue.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<hr>
<h1>Chapter 5 — Vendor Personas</h1>
<h2>Why Understanding Vendors is Existential for ThreadIt</h2>
<p>In a marketplace, <strong>supply creates demand, and demand creates supply.</strong> But for ThreadIt, the chicken-and-egg problem has a clear answer: <strong>we must start with vendors.</strong></p>
<p>Without vendors → no catalog → no products to show → no customer orders → no business.</p>
<p>Without customers → vendors can still sell in-store → they lose nothing → they&#39;ll wait.</p>
<p><strong>This means vendor onboarding is our #1 operational priority</strong> in the pre-launch and launch phases. And vendor onboarding for fashion is significantly harder than for food — here&#39;s why:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Factor</th>
<th>Zomato (Food)</th>
<th>ThreadIt (Fashion)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>Menu size</td>
<td>20–50 items</td>
<td>200–2,000+ SKUs (size × color × style)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Catalog creation</td>
<td>One-time photo shoot, menu updates</td>
<td>Continuous — new stock arrives weekly</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Inventory accuracy</td>
<td>Chef knows what&#39;s available</td>
<td>Store owner may not know exact stock by size/color</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Prep time</td>
<td>10–20 minutes (cooking)</td>
<td>2–5 minutes (locate + pack)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Standardization</td>
<td>&quot;Butter Chicken&quot; is understood</td>
<td>&quot;Slim Fit L&quot; means different things at different stores</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<blockquote>
<p>[!CAUTION]<br><strong>The catalog problem is ThreadIt&#39;s hardest operational challenge.</strong> Getting 200 stores to digitize their entire inventory with accurate photos, sizes, prices, and real-time stock levels is a monumental task. We must solve this with onboarding assistance, not by expecting vendors to do it themselves. This is covered in detail in the Operations chapter.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Vendor Persona 1: Rakesh — The Established Multi-Brand Store Owner</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Store Type</strong></td>
<td>Mid-to-large multi-brand clothing store</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Location</strong></td>
<td>Prime area — Fancy Bazaar, Pan Bazaar, GS Road, Ganeshguri</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Inventory</strong></td>
<td>1,000–5,000+ SKUs across brands, sizes, and categories</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Monthly Revenue</strong></td>
<td>₹3,00,000–₹15,00,000+ (Assumption — based on store size/location)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Staff</strong></td>
<td>3–10 employees</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Digital Presence</strong></td>
<td>Google Maps listing. Maybe a Facebook page with sporadic posts. No online ordering.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Tech Comfort</strong></td>
<td>Uses WhatsApp. Has a billing software (maybe Tally or a basic POS). Not comfortable with complex tech.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>What Rakesh wants:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>More customers without more marketing spend</li>
<li>A way to reach the &quot;online generation&quot; without learning e-commerce himself</li>
<li>Protection from the slow bleed of customers to Myntra/Amazon</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>What Rakesh fears:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>&quot;Will you steal my customers?&quot; — fear that ThreadIt will eventually cut out the middleman</li>
<li>&quot;Who pays for returns?&quot; — he knows fashion returns are expensive</li>
<li>&quot;Will this eat into my margins?&quot; — if commission is too high, his already-thin margins (on branded goods) shrink further</li>
<li>&quot;Will I have to manage another app?&quot; — he&#39;s already overwhelmed with the store</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>How to win Rakesh:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Onboarding must be zero-effort for him.</strong> We send a team to photograph his inventory, input sizes, and set up his catalog. He just approves.</li>
<li><strong>The first order must come within 48 hours of onboarding.</strong> If he lists his store and nothing happens for 2 weeks, he&#39;ll lose interest and stop keeping the app active.</li>
<li><strong>Commission must feel fair.</strong> Rakesh operates on ~30–40% margin for branded goods and ~50–70% for unbranded/local brands. A 15% commission is acceptable on unbranded; it&#39;s tight on branded.</li>
<li><strong>Payouts must be fast.</strong> He operates on cash flow. Weekly settlement minimum; daily is better.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>[!WARNING]<br><strong>Rakesh is the most important vendor but also the hardest to onboard.</strong> His inventory is massive, his time is limited, and his trust must be earned. Losing a Rakesh-type vendor after onboarding is expensive — the catalog investment is wasted. Retention here depends on consistent order volume.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Vendor Persona 2: Deepa — The Boutique / Women&#39;s Specialty Store</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Store Type</strong></td>
<td>Small boutique or women&#39;s ethnic/western wear specialty store</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Location</strong></td>
<td>Side streets near main markets, residential commercial areas, or inside malls</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Inventory</strong></td>
<td>200–800 SKUs, curated collection</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Monthly Revenue</strong></td>
<td>₹80,000–₹3,00,000 (Assumption)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Staff</strong></td>
<td>1–3 (often the owner herself)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Digital Presence</strong></td>
<td>Active on Instagram. Posts regularly. Gets DMs asking for prices. Some WhatsApp-based orders from regulars.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Tech Comfort</strong></td>
<td>Moderate — uses Instagram, WhatsApp Business, maybe Canva for posts</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>What Deepa wants:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>A proper ordering and delivery system — she&#39;s tired of DM-based ordering and unreliable delivery</li>
<li>More reach beyond her Instagram followers</li>
<li>A professional storefront without building a website</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>What Deepa fears:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>&quot;Will my store&#39;s brand get lost on your platform?&quot; — she&#39;s built a reputation around curation</li>
<li>&quot;Will I have to compete on price with bigger stores?&quot; — she can&#39;t match Rakesh&#39;s prices</li>
<li>&quot;What if someone orders and I&#39;m busy with an in-store customer?&quot; — she doesn&#39;t have staff to handle concurrent channels</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>How to win Deepa:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Give her store a branded page</strong> on ThreadIt — her store name, her aesthetic, her collection. Not just a list of products dumped into a generic feed.</li>
<li><strong>Let her set her own prices and control her catalog</strong> — she&#39;s a curator, not a commodity seller</li>
<li><strong>Flexible order acceptance</strong> — let her set &quot;active hours&quot; and pause orders when she&#39;s overwhelmed</li>
<li><strong>Instagram integration potential</strong> — if she can share her ThreadIt store link on Instagram, we become her ordering backend. This is a powerful wedge.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>[!TIP]<br><strong>Deepa is our viral vendor.</strong> She will promote ThreadIt to her Instagram audience because it solves a real problem for her. One Deepa with 5,000 Instagram followers is worth more than 10 Rakeshes for customer acquisition. Prioritize Deepa-type vendors in the early phase for organic demand generation.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Vendor Persona 3: Manish — The Wholesale-Turned-Retail Shop</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Store Type</strong></td>
<td>Primarily wholesale, does some retail. Men&#39;s basics — shirts, trousers, t-shirts, undergarments.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Location</strong></td>
<td>Fancy Bazaar, wholesale lanes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Inventory</strong></td>
<td>Massive — 5,000–20,000+ SKUs but limited variety (many of the same item in different sizes)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Monthly Revenue</strong></td>
<td>₹5,00,000–₹25,00,000+ (primarily wholesale)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Staff</strong></td>
<td>5–15</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Digital Presence</strong></td>
<td>Almost none. Maybe an IndiaMart listing.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Tech Comfort</strong></td>
<td>Low. Uses basic billing software.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>What Manish wants:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>A retail channel without retail effort — he&#39;s a wholesaler at heart</li>
<li>Move slow-selling retail inventory faster</li>
<li>Compete with Meesho, which is eating into his wholesale business</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>What Manish fears:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Nothing, really. Retail is a small part of his business. He&#39;ll try ThreadIt if it&#39;s low effort, but won&#39;t invest energy into it.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>How to win Manish:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Bundle onboarding with Rakesh-type stores</strong> — if we&#39;re already in Fancy Bazaar photographing stores, we add Manish</li>
<li><strong>Start with his best-selling basics</strong> — don&#39;t try to catalog 20,000 SKUs. List his top 100 items.</li>
<li><strong>Position ThreadIt as incremental revenue</strong> — even 10 extra retail orders/day at ₹500 AOV is ₹1,50,000/month he wasn&#39;t getting</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Why Manish matters:</strong> His prices are the lowest. He can be our <strong>&quot;value&quot; option</strong> — when a customer searches for &quot;black formal trousers,&quot; Manish&#39;s ₹600 option appears alongside Rakesh&#39;s ₹1,400 branded option. This gives customers choice and makes the platform feel comprehensive.</p>
<h2>Vendor Persona 4: Kabir — The New/Struggling Store</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Store Type</strong></td>
<td>Recently opened (&lt; 2 years), small, undifferentiated</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Location</strong></td>
<td>Newer commercial areas, secondary locations</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Inventory</strong></td>
<td>100–400 SKUs, inconsistent quality</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Monthly Revenue</strong></td>
<td>₹30,000–₹1,00,000 (struggling)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Staff</strong></td>
<td>1–2 (owner + maybe 1 helper)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Digital Presence</strong></td>
<td>Tried everything — Instagram, Facebook, Google Ads — nothing worked</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Tech Comfort</strong></td>
<td>High (often younger, entered retail recently)</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>What Kabir wants:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Customers. Period. He&#39;s desperate.</li>
<li>Any platform that brings him orders is his best friend.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>What Kabir fears:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Nothing. He has nothing to lose.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Assessment:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>[!WARNING]<br><strong>Kabir is eager but dangerous.</strong> He&#39;ll sign up immediately, but his catalog quality may be poor, his products inconsistent, and his store experience for customers mediocre. If a customer&#39;s first ThreadIt order comes from Kabir and it&#39;s disappointing, we lose that customer forever — and they blame the platform, not the store. <strong>We must have quality gates for vendor onboarding.</strong> Not every store that wants to join should be allowed to join in v1.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Vendor Prioritization Matrix</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Priority</th>
<th>Persona</th>
<th>Reason</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>P0 — Onboard First</strong></td>
<td>Deepa (Boutique)</td>
<td>Viral potential, curated catalog, Instagram audience, manageable catalog size</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>P1 — Onboard Early</strong></td>
<td>Rakesh (Established Store)</td>
<td>Catalog depth, trust, credibility for the platform</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>P2 — Onboard for Breadth</strong></td>
<td>Manish (Wholesale-Retail)</td>
<td>Price competitiveness, basics category coverage</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>P3 — Onboard Cautiously</strong></td>
<td>Kabir (New/Struggling)</td>
<td>Only after quality vetting; reject if catalog quality is below threshold</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>The Vendor Adoption Funnel</h2>
<pre><code>AWARENESS       → Store owner hears about ThreadIt
                   (via our field team, word of mouth, vendor referrals)
                   
CONSIDERATION   → &quot;What&#39;s in it for me? What does it cost?&quot;
                   (field team pitch, show demo, explain commission)
                   
ONBOARDING      → Our team catalogs their inventory, sets up store
                   (CRITICAL: this must be done FOR them, not BY them)
                   
FIRST ORDER     → First order received and fulfilled
                   (must happen within 48 hours of going live)
                   
REGULAR USAGE   → 5+ orders/week consistently
                   (this is when the vendor becomes &quot;retained&quot;)
                   
ADVOCACY        → Vendor recommends ThreadIt to other store owners
                   (the ultimate signal — supply-side virality)
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Key drop-off points:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Awareness → Consideration:</strong> &quot;Sounds too good to be true&quot; / &quot;I&#39;m too busy to try something new&quot;</li>
<li><strong>Onboarding → First Order:</strong> If no orders come, vendor goes cold. <strong>This is the biggest risk.</strong> We need guaranteed early demand through promotional offers, our own team placing orders, or targeted customer acquisition in the vendor&#39;s zone.</li>
<li><strong>First Order → Regular Usage:</strong> If order volume is inconsistent, vendor stops keeping the app active. Products show as &quot;available&quot; but when an order comes, the vendor hasn&#39;t updated stock — leading to cancellations.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>[!IMPORTANT]<br><strong>The onboarding-to-first-order gap is the vendor churn death zone.</strong> Every vendor who goes live and gets no orders in the first week is a vendor who will badmouth us to other store owners. In the pilot, we should <strong>only onboard vendors in zones where we have already started customer acquisition.</strong> Don&#39;t spray vendors across the city — cluster them in launch zones.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Vendor Resistance Map</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Objection</th>
<th>Response Strategy</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>&quot;I don&#39;t have time for this&quot;</td>
<td>&quot;We do everything for you. Our team comes to your store, photographs products, and sets up your catalog. You just approve and receive orders.&quot;</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&quot;15% commission is too high&quot;</td>
<td>&quot;What&#39;s your cost of acquiring a new customer today? Your rent on this store — divided by footfall — is your customer acquisition cost. We bring you customers at zero upfront cost.&quot;</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&quot;What about returns?&quot;</td>
<td>&quot;Customers are ordering from nearby stores. They know your store, they trust your brand. Our data shows (Assumption — to be validated) that return rates for hyperlocal orders are significantly lower than Myntra/Amazon.&quot;</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&quot;I already have enough customers&quot;</td>
<td>&quot;You have enough walk-in customers today. But the 18–30 age group is shifting online. In 3 years, what percentage of your footfall will you have lost?&quot;</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&quot;My products are premium — I don&#39;t want to be on a discount app&quot;</td>
<td>&quot;ThreadIt is not a discount platform. You set your own prices. Your store page showcases your brand. Think of us as your own delivery-enabled website.&quot;</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
