<h3>Rider Reality Check</h3>
<blockquote>
<p>[!WARNING]<br><strong>The rider value proposition is our weakest side in the early phase.</strong> Here&#39;s why:</p>
<ul>
<li>Fashion orders are <strong>less frequent</strong> than food orders. A Zomato rider can do 15–20 deliveries/day. If ThreadIt has 50 orders/day across the city, individual rider utilization will be very low.</li>
<li>Low utilization = low earnings. Low earnings = rider churn.</li>
<li>We <strong>cannot build a dedicated rider fleet</strong> until order volume justifies it.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Proposed solution for v1:</strong> Partner with existing gig delivery platforms (Dunzo, Porter, local logistics partners) rather than building our own rider network. This converts fixed rider costs into variable per-order costs and solves the cold-start problem. We build our own fleet only when we hit a threshold where dedicated riders become cheaper than third-party partners. This trade-off is analyzed in Unit Economics.</p>
</blockquote>
