<hr>
<h1>Chapter 9 — Order Lifecycle</h1>
<h2>Why This Chapter Matters</h2>
<p>The order lifecycle is the operational heartbeat of ThreadIt. Every order that flows through the system touches all three sides of the marketplace — customer, vendor, and rider. Getting any step wrong means a failed delivery, a refund, and potential churn.</p>
<p><strong>This chapter serves as the operating spec for:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Product/engineering team (to build the right state machine)</li>
<li>Operations team (to handle edge cases)</li>
<li>Customer support (to know what went wrong and when)</li>
</ul>
<h2>The Complete Order State Machine</h2>
<pre><code>┌─────────┐    ┌───────────┐    ┌───────────┐    ┌──────────┐
│ BROWSE  │───▶│   CART    │───▶│  PAYMENT  │───▶│  ORDER   │
│         │    │           │    │ INITIATED │    │  PLACED  │
└─────────┘    └───────────┘    └───────────┘    └────┬─────┘
                                                      │
                                          ┌───────────┴───────────┐
                                          ▼                       ▼
                                   ┌──────────┐           ┌──────────────┐
                                   │ VENDOR   │           │   VENDOR     │
                                   │ ACCEPTED │           │   REJECTED   │
                                   └────┬─────┘           └──────┬───────┘
                                        │                        │
                                        ▼                        ▼
                                   ┌──────────┐           ┌──────────────┐
                                   │ PACKING  │           │  REROUTE /   │
                                   │          │           │  REFUND      │
                                   └────┬─────┘           └──────────────┘
                                        │
                                        ▼
                                   ┌──────────┐
                                   │  RIDER   │
                                   │ ASSIGNED │
                                   └────┬─────┘
                                        │
                                        ▼
                                   ┌──────────┐
                                   │  PICKED  │
                                   │    UP    │
                                   └────┬─────┘
                                        │
                                        ▼
                                   ┌──────────┐
                                   │   IN     │
                                   │ TRANSIT  │
                                   └────┬─────┘
                                        │
                                        ▼
                                   ┌──────────┐
                                   │DELIVERED │
                                   └────┬─────┘
                                        │
                              ┌─────────┴─────────┐
                              ▼                   ▼
                        ┌──────────┐        ┌──────────┐
                        │COMPLETED │        │ RETURN / │
                        │          │        │ EXCHANGE │
                        └──────────┘        └──────────┘
</code></pre>
<h2>State-by-State Breakdown</h2>
<h3>State 1: BROWSE</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>What happens</strong></td>
<td>Customer opens app, sees nearby stores and products based on location</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Key data</strong></td>
<td>Customer GPS location, store locations, product catalog, availability status</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Customer sees</strong></td>
<td>Store cards sorted by distance/rating, product listings with photos/price/size, store ratings</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>System does</strong></td>
<td>Geo-query for stores within delivery radius (default: 5 km), filter by open/active status, rank by relevance</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Failure mode</strong></td>
<td>No stores nearby, all stores closed, empty catalog → show &quot;No stores available in your area&quot; with waitlist option</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Design decision:</strong> Should we show products first (like Myntra — product-centric browse) or stores first (like Zomato — store-centric browse)?</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Approach</th>
<th>Pros</th>
<th>Cons</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Store-first</strong> (like Zomato)</td>
<td>Builds store trust, leverages existing store relationships, vendor branding preserved</td>
<td>Harder to compare products across stores, customer may not know which store to pick</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Product-first</strong> (like Myntra)</td>
<td>Familiar e-commerce UX, enables cross-store comparison, better for category search (&quot;black formal shirt&quot;)</td>
<td>Reduces store identity, makes vendors feel commoditized, harder to build store loyalty</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Hybrid</strong></td>
<td>Best of both — category search shows products from multiple stores, but each product clearly shows which store it&#39;s from</td>
<td>More complex UI, requires careful design</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Recommendation: Hybrid, leaning store-first.</strong></p>
<p>The home screen shows nearby stores (like Zomato). But search and category pages show products across stores (like Myntra). Each product card prominently displays the store name, rating, and distance. This gives customers both discovery modes while preserving vendor identity.</p>
<h3>State 2: CART</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>What happens</strong></td>
<td>Customer adds items to cart</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Key constraint</strong></td>
<td><strong>Single-store cart in v1.</strong> Customer can only order from one store at a time. Multi-store carts require split orders, multiple riders, and complex logistics. Not in MVP.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Customer sees</strong></td>
<td>Cart with items, sizes, quantities, subtotal, estimated delivery fee, estimated delivery time</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Validation</strong></td>
<td>If customer adds items from a different store, prompt: &quot;You have items from [Store A]. Adding from [Store B] will start a new cart.&quot;</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<blockquote>
<p>[!IMPORTANT]<br><strong>Single-store cart is a deliberate v1 limitation, not a bug.</strong> Multi-store ordering requires dispatching multiple riders or complex route optimization. It also creates a confusing returns experience. We add multi-store ordering only after single-store order lifecycle is rock solid.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>State 3: PAYMENT INITIATED</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>What happens</strong></td>
<td>Customer proceeds to checkout and initiates payment</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Payment methods (v1)</strong></td>
<td>UPI (PhonePe, GPay, Paytm), Debit/Credit Card, COD (Cash on Delivery)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>COD consideration</strong></td>
<td>COD increases conversion but also increases fraud, no-shows, and operational complexity. Offer COD only on orders below ₹2,000 and for verified customers (2+ completed orders). New users: first order must be prepaid.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Payment gateway</strong></td>
<td>Razorpay or Cashfree (both support UPI, cards, and have split payout features for marketplace)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Timeout</strong></td>
<td>Payment must complete within 10 minutes or cart is released</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>COD Policy — Detailed:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Customer Type</th>
<th>COD Available?</th>
<th>Rationale</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>First-time customer</td>
<td>❌ No</td>
<td>High risk of fraud/no-show. Must establish trust with prepaid first order.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1 completed order</td>
<td>❌ No</td>
<td>Not enough history</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2+ completed orders, no issues</td>
<td>✅ Yes, up to ₹2,000</td>
<td>Proven reliable customer</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Customer with previous COD refusal/RTO</td>
<td>❌ No</td>
<td>COD privilege revoked</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>State 4: ORDER PLACED</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>What happens</strong></td>
<td>Payment succeeds. Order is created in the system. Vendor is notified.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>The clock starts… partially.</strong></td>
<td>Customer sees &quot;Order placed! Waiting for store confirmation.&quot; The 30-minute delivery promise does NOT start yet.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Vendor notification</strong></td>
<td>Push notification + SMS + in-app alert + sound alarm on vendor app. Must be impossible to miss.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>SLA</strong></td>
<td>Vendor must confirm or reject within <strong>3 minutes</strong>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Auto-reject</strong></td>
<td>If vendor doesn&#39;t respond within 3 minutes → order is auto-cancelled, customer is notified, system attempts to find the same product at another nearby store.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<blockquote>
<p>[!IMPORTANT]<br><strong>The 3-minute vendor confirmation window is the most fragile point in the entire system.</strong> If the vendor&#39;s phone is on silent, if they&#39;re attending to an in-store customer, if the app crashed — the order fails. <strong>Backup: SMS + automated phone call after 1 minute of no response.</strong> &quot;You have a new ThreadIt order! Open the app to confirm.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>State 5: VENDOR ACCEPTED</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>What happens</strong></td>
<td>Vendor confirms the item is available and starts packing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>🕐 The 30-minute clock starts NOW.</strong></td>
<td>Customer sees: &quot;Your order is confirmed! Estimated delivery in 28 minutes.&quot;</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Vendor action</strong></td>
<td>Locate the item, verify size/color, pack it in a ThreadIt-branded bag (we supply these), mark &quot;Ready for Pickup&quot;</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>SLA</strong></td>
<td>Pack and mark &quot;Ready&quot; within <strong>5 minutes</strong></td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>State 6: VENDOR REJECTED</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>What happens</strong></td>
<td>Vendor rejects the order — item is out of stock, wrong listing, or store is too busy</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Customer experience</strong></td>
<td>&quot;Unfortunately, [Store Name] couldn&#39;t fulfill your order. [Reason].&quot;</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>System action</strong></td>
<td>Automatically search for the same/similar product at other nearby stores. If found, offer to re-route. If not, full refund.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Refund SLA</strong></td>
<td>Instant refund to original payment method (Razorpay supports instant refunds for UPI).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Vendor impact</strong></td>
<td>Rejection is logged. Vendors with rejection rate &gt; 15% get a warning. &gt; 25% triggers account review. &gt; 40% results in temporary delisting.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>State 7: PACKING</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>What happens</strong></td>
<td>Vendor locates and packs the product</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Packaging</strong></td>
<td>ThreadIt-branded paper/cloth bags (eco-friendly — potential brand differentiator). We supply these. Store inserts a thank-you card with the store&#39;s name (not ThreadIt&#39;s).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Quality check</strong></td>
<td>Vendor is responsible for checking the item matches the order (right size, right color, no defects). We can&#39;t enforce this in v1 — post-delivery complaints and vendor ratings are the feedback loop.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>SLA</strong></td>
<td>Max 5 minutes from acceptance to &quot;Ready for Pickup&quot;</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Failure mode</strong></td>
<td>Vendor accepts but then can&#39;t find the item → must mark &quot;Packing Issue&quot; → customer is notified → refund initiated</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>State 8: RIDER ASSIGNED</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>What happens</strong></td>
<td>System assigns the nearest available rider (or dispatches request to third-party logistics partner)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Timing</strong></td>
<td>Rider assignment should happen <strong>in parallel with vendor packing</strong>, not after. When vendor accepts the order, the system simultaneously starts finding a rider.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Assignment logic</strong></td>
<td>Nearest available rider → estimated time to store + estimated time to customer must be within remaining delivery window</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Customer sees</strong></td>
<td>&quot;Rider assigned! [Name] is heading to pick up your order.&quot; + live map tracking</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Failure mode</strong></td>
<td>No rider available → ops team alert → backup: call local rider directly or expand radius</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>State 9: PICKED UP</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>What happens</strong></td>
<td>Rider arrives at store, collects the package, confirms pickup in app</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Verification</strong></td>
<td>Rider scans a QR code / enters an OTP generated by the vendor app to confirm correct order pickup. Prevents wrong-order pickup.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>SLA</strong></td>
<td>Pickup must happen within 10 minutes of vendor marking &quot;Ready&quot;</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Customer sees</strong></td>
<td>&quot;Your order has been picked up! [Rider Name] is on the way.&quot;</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>State 10: IN TRANSIT</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>What happens</strong></td>
<td>Rider is en route to customer&#39;s delivery address</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Customer sees</strong></td>
<td>Live map tracking of rider&#39;s location + ETA</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Rider action</strong></td>
<td>Follow navigation, contact customer if address is unclear</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>SLA</strong></td>
<td>Deliver within 15 minutes of pickup (for orders within 5 km radius)</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>State 11: DELIVERED</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>What happens</strong></td>
<td>Rider arrives at delivery location, hands over package</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Confirmation</strong></td>
<td>Customer confirms delivery via OTP (for prepaid) or hands over cash (for COD). Rider marks &quot;Delivered&quot; in app.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>COD handling</strong></td>
<td>Rider collects exact amount. Any shortage is the rider&#39;s liability. Cash reconciliation at end of day.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Customer action</strong></td>
<td>Customer is prompted to rate the experience (product quality, delivery speed, rider behavior)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>SLA</strong></td>
<td>Total time from vendor confirmation to delivery: ≤ 30 minutes</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>State 12: COMPLETED / RETURN / EXCHANGE</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Completed</strong></td>
<td>No issues. Customer is happy. Order is finalized. Vendor payout is processed in next settlement cycle.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Return window</strong></td>
<td>24 hours from delivery to initiate a return. (Detailed in next chapter.)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Exchange</strong></td>
<td>Customer requests size/color exchange. Same-store exchange only. A new pickup-and-delivery is initiated.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>The 30-Minute Delivery Budget</h2>
<p>Every minute counts. Here&#39;s how the 30-minute window is allocated:</p>
<pre><code>TOTAL WINDOW: 30 MINUTES (from vendor acceptance to delivery)

┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ 0     2     5          10         15         20         25         30  min  │
│ ├─────┼─────┼──────────┼──────────┼──────────┼──────────┼──────────┤       │
│ │ORDER│PACK │  RIDER   │  RIDER   │          │          │          │       │
│ │PROC.│     │  TRAVEL  │  PICKUP  │   RIDER DELIVERY TRANSIT     │       │
│ │     │     │ TO STORE │  + VERIFY│                               │       │
│ ├─────┼─────┼──────────┼──────────┼───────────────────────────────┤       │
│  0-2   2-5     5-10      10-12             12-30                          │
│  min   min     min        min               min                           │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Breakdown:
├── Order Processing + Vendor Accept:   0-2  min  (already done before clock starts)
├── Vendor Packing:                     0-5  min  
├── Rider Dispatched (parallel):        0-3  min  (happens during packing)
├── Rider Travels to Store:             3-10 min  (depends on rider proximity)
├── Rider Pickup + Verify:             10-12 min  
├── Rider Delivers to Customer:        12-30 min  (depends on distance)
└── BUFFER:                             ~3-5 min  (weather, traffic, address confusion)
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>[!WARNING]<br><strong>The tightest constraint is the RIDER TRAVEL TO STORE segment.</strong> If the nearest rider is 4 km from the store, that&#39;s 10–12 minutes just for the rider to reach the store — eating half the delivery window before the customer&#39;s product even leaves the shop. This is why <strong>rider positioning near high-density vendor zones</strong> is critical. Pre-positioning even 2–3 riders near Fancy Bazaar/Pan Bazaar during peak hours can save 5–8 minutes per order.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Edge Cases — The Stuff That Actually Breaks</h2>
<p>These are the scenarios that don&#39;t appear in happy-path demos but happen daily in real operations:</p>
<h3>Edge Case 1: Vendor Accepts, Then Can&#39;t Find the Item</h3>
<pre><code>Trigger:     Vendor hits &quot;Accept&quot; without checking → goes to find the item → it&#39;s sold out
Impact:      Customer is waiting, clock is ticking, rider might already be dispatched
Handling:    
  1. Vendor marks &quot;Packing Issue — Item Unavailable&quot;
  2. Customer notified: &quot;Sorry, the item couldn&#39;t be found at the store.&quot;
  3. System checks other stores for same product → offer reroute
  4. If no alternative: full refund (instant for UPI, 5-7 days for card)
  5. Vendor receives a &quot;false acceptance&quot; strike
  6. Rider (if dispatched) receives a cancellation fee (₹15)
</code></pre>
<h3>Edge Case 2: Partial Availability (Multi-Item Order)</h3>
<pre><code>Trigger:     Customer orders 3 items from one store. 2 are available, 1 is not.
Impact:      Partial fulfillment creates pricing/refund complexity
Handling:    
  1. Vendor marks which items are available and which aren&#39;t
  2. Customer sees: &quot;2 of 3 items available. Proceed with available items?&quot;
  3. Customer can: 
     a. Accept partial order (refund for unavailable item)
     b. Cancel entire order (full refund)
  4. Delivery fee is NOT reduced for partial orders (same logistics cost)
</code></pre>
<h3>Edge Case 3: Rider No-Show</h3>
<pre><code>Trigger:     Rider accepts order but doesn&#39;t show up at store within SLA
Impact:      Vendor has a packed order sitting, customer is waiting
Handling:    
  1. After 8 minutes of no movement, system sends reminder to rider
  2. After 12 minutes, auto-reassign to next available rider
  3. Customer notified: &quot;Your order is being reassigned to a new rider. Sorry for the delay.&quot;
  4. Original rider receives a penalty (reduced future order priority)
  5. Updated ETA shown to customer
</code></pre>
<h3>Edge Case 4: Customer Unreachable at Delivery</h3>
<pre><code>Trigger:     Rider arrives at address, customer doesn&#39;t respond to calls/doorbell
Impact:      Rider is stuck, other deliveries are delayed
Handling:    
  1. Rider calls customer (2 attempts, 2-minute gap)
  2. Rider calls customer support
  3. Timer: 5-minute wait at door
  4. After 5 minutes: mark as &quot;Delivery Failed — Customer Unreachable&quot;
  5. Product returned to store (rider takes it back)
  6. Customer charged a &quot;return-to-store&quot; fee of ₹50
  7. Prepaid: refund minus return fee. COD: no charge collected, but customer flagged.
</code></pre>
<h3>Edge Case 5: Wrong Item Delivered</h3>
<pre><code>Trigger:     Customer opens package, finds different item than what they ordered
Impact:      Trust destroyed, immediate negative reaction
Handling:    
  1. Customer reports via app (photo required — shows what was received)
  2. Instant new pickup scheduled — rider picks up wrong item from customer
  3. Correct item dispatched from store (if available), OR full refund
  4. Vendor receives a &quot;wrong item&quot; strike (3 strikes → mandatory quality review)
  5. Customer receives a ₹50 ThreadIt credit as apology
  6. No charges to customer for any part of this
</code></pre>
<h3>Edge Case 6: Payment Fails After Order Placed</h3>
<pre><code>Trigger:     Rare but possible — payment appears successful, bank later declines
Impact:      Order is in process but payment didn&#39;t actually clear
Handling:    
  1. Payment gateway webhook confirms failure
  2. If order hasn&#39;t been accepted by vendor yet → auto-cancel
  3. If vendor has already packed → CRITICAL SITUATION
     - Attempt payment retry (customer notified)
     - If retry fails: order cancelled, vendor notified, product returned to shelf
     - Vendor receives a &quot;cancellation fee&quot; from ThreadIt (₹25) as compensation
  4. Customer account flagged for payment reliability
</code></pre>
<h3>Edge Case 7: Store Closes During Active Order</h3>
<pre><code>Trigger:     Vendor goes offline / closes store while an order is being processed
Impact:      Depends on what state the order is in
Handling:    
  - If PLACED (not yet accepted): auto-reject, reroute to other stores
  - If ACCEPTED but not packed: contact vendor (call). If unreachable within 2 min, cancel + refund
  - If PACKED and rider assigned: rider still picks up (store staff should leave it accessible)
  - If PACKED and no rider: cancel + refund, vendor strike for mid-order closure
</code></pre>
<h3>Edge Case 8: Customer Wants to Modify Order After Placing</h3>
<pre><code>Trigger:     Customer wants to change size, add an item, or swap a product
Impact:      Modifying a live order disrupts the fulfillment pipeline
Handling:    
  - If ORDER PLACED (vendor hasn&#39;t accepted yet): allow modification
  - If VENDOR ACCEPTED or later: no modification allowed. Customer must:
    a. Let current order complete, then return/exchange, OR
    b. Cancel order (cancellation fee may apply if vendor already packed)
  - v1 rule: Order modification is NOT supported post-acceptance. Keep it simple.
</code></pre>
