<h1>Chapter 10 — Returns &amp; Exchange Policy</h1>
<h2>Why This Chapter Gets Its Own Section</h2>
<p>Returns are where fashion marketplaces go to die.</p>
<p>Every fashion e-commerce company in India — Myntra, Ajio, Meesho — bleeds money on returns. Return rates of 25–40% are normal. Each return costs the platform ₹80–₹150 in reverse logistics, refund processing, and product handling.</p>
<p><strong>ThreadIt&#39;s returns policy is not just a customer service document — it&#39;s a unit economics survival mechanism.</strong> Get it wrong, and every order we process loses money. Get it right, and our hyperlocal model gives us a structural advantage that national platforms can never match.</p>
<h2>The Core Philosophy: Exchanges Over Returns, Always</h2>
<pre><code>┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                                                            │
│   RETURN = Money leaves the system.                        │
│            Customer is refunded. Vendor gets product back. │
│            ThreadIt earns zero. Everyone loses.            │
│                                                            │
│   EXCHANGE = Money stays in the system.                    │
│              Customer gets the right product.              │
│              Vendor makes the sale.                        │
│              ThreadIt keeps the commission.                │
│              Everyone wins.                                │
│                                                            │
│   ➜ Every policy decision should NUDGE toward exchange.    │
│                                                            │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Why ThreadIt has a structural exchange advantage:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Factor</th>
<th>National Platform (Myntra)</th>
<th>ThreadIt (Hyperlocal)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>Exchange logistics</td>
<td>Ship back → warehouse → inspect → ship new item = 7–14 days</td>
<td>Rider picks up old item + delivers new item from same store = 60–90 minutes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Customer willingness to exchange</td>
<td>Low — too slow, just refund me</td>
<td>High — I can get the right size in an hour</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Vendor involvement in exchange</td>
<td>Zero — warehouse handles everything</td>
<td>Direct — vendor knows what sizes are in stock, can recommend</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Exchange cost</td>
<td>High — two full shipping cycles</td>
<td>Low — one rider trip, short distance</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>This is perhaps ThreadIt&#39;s biggest hidden advantage.</strong> National platforms can&#39;t do fast exchanges. We can. This means our effective return rate (money-out-of-system returns) should be significantly lower than our total return-initiation rate.</p>
<h2>Return Reason Categories</h2>
<p>Not all returns are equal. Different reasons get different treatment:</p>
<h3>Category 1: Size/Fit Issue</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>What happened</strong></td>
<td>Product doesn&#39;t fit — too big, too small, wrong fit</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Expected frequency</strong></td>
<td>Highest — this is the #1 return reason in fashion</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>ThreadIt&#39;s response</strong></td>
<td><strong>Exchange first.</strong> &quot;Would you like a different size? We can have it delivered in 60 minutes.&quot;</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>If exchange</strong></td>
<td>Free. Rider picks up wrong size and delivers correct size from same store in one trip. No additional delivery fee.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>If return (customer insists)</strong></td>
<td>Accepted. ₹49 return shipping fee deducted from refund. This is not to punish — it&#39;s to cover the reverse logistics cost and nudge toward exchange.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Vendor impact</strong></td>
<td>No penalty. Size issues are expected in fashion, especially without try-on.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>Category 2: Defective / Damaged Product</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>What happened</strong></td>
<td>Product has a manufacturing defect, is damaged, or is soiled</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Expected frequency</strong></td>
<td>Low (&lt; 2% of orders) if vendor quality vetting is done properly</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>ThreadIt&#39;s response</strong></td>
<td><strong>Immediate free return OR exchange.</strong> Customer chooses. No questions asked.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>If return</strong></td>
<td>Full refund, zero fees. ThreadIt absorbs all costs.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>If exchange</strong></td>
<td>Free replacement from same store. Priority delivery.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Vendor impact</strong></td>
<td>Vendor absorbs the cost of the defective product. If defect rate &gt; 5%, vendor flagged for quality review. Repeat offenders face listing restrictions.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Evidence required</strong></td>
<td>Customer submits photo of defect. Reviewed by support within 15 minutes. Auto-approved for clear defects.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>Category 3: Not As Described</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>What happened</strong></td>
<td>Product received doesn&#39;t match the listing — wrong color, different material, different brand/style than photographed</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Expected frequency</strong></td>
<td>Should be rare if catalog photography is accurate. Higher frequency = catalog quality problem.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>ThreadIt&#39;s response</strong></td>
<td><strong>Free return, no questions.</strong> This is a platform failure — the listing was wrong.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Vendor impact</strong></td>
<td>Vendor receives a &quot;listing accuracy&quot; strike. Product listing is flagged for review. If systemic (multiple items), catalog team re-photographs the store&#39;s products.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>ThreadIt&#39;s cost</strong></td>
<td>Full reverse logistics cost absorbed. If this category exceeds 3% of orders, it&#39;s a catalog crisis — trigger an emergency catalog audit.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>Category 4: Change of Mind / No Longer Needed</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>What happened</strong></td>
<td>Customer simply doesn&#39;t want it anymore. No defect, right size, matches listing.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Expected frequency</strong></td>
<td>Medium — impulse purchases, found something better, event cancelled</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>ThreadIt&#39;s response</strong></td>
<td><strong>Return accepted with conditions.</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Conditions</strong></td>
<td>Product must be unused (tags attached, no wash, no wear). ₹49 return fee deducted from refund. Return pickup within 24 hours.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Vendor impact</strong></td>
<td>No penalty. Product returned to store in original condition.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>The Return Window: 24 Hours</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Window</strong></td>
<td>24 hours from delivery confirmation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Why 24 hours (not 7 or 30 days)?</strong></td>
<td>We&#39;re hyperlocal — the store is 3 km away. You don&#39;t need a week to decide if a shirt fits. 24 hours gives you time to try it on at home, show your family, decide. It also limits wear-and-return fraud.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Exchange window</strong></td>
<td>48 hours (longer because exchange keeps the sale alive)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Exception</strong></td>
<td>Defective products: 72-hour window (defects may not be immediately visible — a seam might tear after first wear)</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<blockquote>
<p>[!NOTE]<br><strong>Why is the exchange window longer than the return window?</strong> Because an exchange is better for everyone — the customer gets what they want, the vendor keeps the sale, and ThreadIt keeps the commission. We want to make exchanges as easy and frictionless as possible, even if the customer discovers the size issue on day 2.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>The Exchange Process — Step by Step</h2>
<p>This is where ThreadIt&#39;s hyperlocal model shines:</p>
<pre><code>CUSTOMER initiates exchange
    │
    ▼
&quot;What would you like instead?&quot;
    │
    ├── Same product, different size → Check store availability
    │                                      │
    │                                 ┌────┴────┐
    │                                 │Available│ → Schedule one-trip swap
    │                                 │         │   (rider picks up old +
    │                                 │         │    delivers new)
    │                                 ├─────────┤
    │                                 │Not in   │ → Check other nearby stores
    │                                 │stock    │   → If found: new store delivery
    │                                 │         │   → If not: offer refund
    │                                 └─────────┘
    │
    ├── Same product, different color → Same flow as above
    │
    └── Different product entirely → Not an exchange — this is a return + new order.
        Customer must return first, then place new order separately.
</code></pre>
<p><strong>The One-Trip Swap:</strong></p>
<p>This is the killer feature. Instead of two separate logistics operations (pickup old item → then deliver new item), we do it in ONE trip:</p>
<ol>
<li>Rider picks up the new item from the store</li>
<li>Rider goes to customer&#39;s address</li>
<li>Customer hands over the old item, receives new item</li>
<li>Rider drops off old item at the store on the way back</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Total time: ~45–60 minutes from exchange request to completion.</strong></p>
<p>Compare this to Myntra where an exchange takes 7–14 days. This speed difference is a massive competitive advantage.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>[!WARNING]<br><strong>The one-trip swap only works if the replacement item is in stock.</strong> If it&#39;s not, the customer has to wait for the old item to be picked up (refund initiated) and then place a new order from a different store. This is a poorer experience, and we should track &quot;exchange fulfillment rate&quot; (percentage of exchange requests successfully completed) as a key metric. Target: &gt; 80%.</p>
</blockquote>
