<td>ThreadIt Wallet (future)</td>
<td>Instant</td>
<td>Credit to wallet</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<blockquote>
<p>[!TIP]<br><strong>Offer &quot;Instant Refund to ThreadIt Wallet&quot; as the default option.</strong> If the customer accepts wallet credit instead of bank refund, we keep the money in our ecosystem, the customer gets instant resolution, and they&#39;re more likely to place another order using the wallet balance. This is standard practice at Myntra and Flipkart. Not for MVP — add in v1.1.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Fraud Prevention</h2>
<p>Fashion returns are ripe for abuse. Common fraud patterns:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Fraud Type</th>
<th>Description</th>
<th>Detection</th>
<th>Response</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Wardrobing</strong></td>
<td>Customer wears the item to an event, then returns it</td>
<td>Photo check for signs of use (wrinkles, deodorant marks, missing tags)</td>
<td>First offense: warning. Second: return privilege restricted.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Tag swapping</strong></td>
<td>Customer puts tags from new item onto old/cheaper item and returns</td>
<td>Weight check (if scale available), visual inspection by vendor on return</td>
<td>Immediate account suspension pending review</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Serial returner</strong></td>
<td>Customer returns &gt; 40% of orders consistently</td>
<td>Automated flag when return rate exceeds threshold</td>
<td>Progressive action: lose free returns → lose COD → account review</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Fake &quot;not as described&quot;</strong></td>
<td>Customer falsely claims product doesn&#39;t match listing to get free return</td>
<td>Compare customer photos with catalog listing</td>
<td>If listing is accurate and product matches, return fee applied</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Fraud Prevention Tiers:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Customer Return Rate</th>
<th>Action</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>0–20%</td>
<td>Normal — no action</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>21–35%</td>
<td>⚠️ Flag for monitoring. No automated action yet.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>36–50%</td>
<td>🟡 Restricted: COD removed, return fees applied on all returns (no free returns even for exchanges)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>50%+</td>
<td>🔴 Account review. Possible suspension. Manual review of every return request.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Financial Impact of Returns — Modeled</h2>
<p><strong>Scenario: 100 orders, 15% return rate, ₹1,200 AOV</strong></p>
<pre><code>Total orders:                    100
Orders with return initiated:     15  (15%)
  ├── Converted to exchange:       9  (60% of returns — our target)
  ├── Actual money-out returns:    6  (40% of returns)
  └── Breakdown of returns:
      ├── Size/fit (₹49 fee):     3
      ├── Defective (free):       1
      ├── Not as described (free): 1
      └── Change of mind (₹49):   1

COST OF RETURNS:
  Per return:
    - Payment gateway fee (already lost):     ₹24.70
    - Rider payout (pickup):                  ₹35
    - Return fee collected (avg):            -₹29.40  (weighted by free vs paid returns)
    - Commission refunded to vendor:          ₹180 (we lose this revenue)
  
  Net cost per return to ThreadIt:            ₹210.30 - ₹29.40 = ~₹180.90
                                              (but ₹180 of this is revenue we simply don&#39;t earn,
                                               not cash out of pocket)
  
  Cash cost per return (out of pocket):       ₹24.70 + ₹35 - ₹29.40 = ₹30.30

TOTAL RETURN IMPACT ON 100 ORDERS:
  Cash cost: 6 returns × ₹30.30 = ₹181.80
  Spread across all 100 orders: ₹1.82/order (negligible)
  
  REVENUE IMPACT:
  Lost commission: 6 × ₹180 = ₹1,080
  Spread across 100 orders: ₹10.80/order in lost potential revenue

NET IMPACT PER ORDER: ~₹12.62/order
(₹1.82 cash + ₹10.80 revenue loss)
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>[!NOTE]<br><strong>At a 15% return rate with 60% exchange conversion, the net cash impact is ~₹1.82/order — very manageable.</strong> The revenue loss from lost commission is more significant at ~₹10.80/order. The critical lever is the exchange conversion rate — every return we convert to an exchange saves us ~₹180 in lost commission. <strong>This is why the one-trip swap must be fast, free, and frictionless.</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<hr>
<h1>Chapter 11 — Operations</h1>
<h2>The Operational Pillars</h2>
<p>ThreadIt&#39;s operations can be broken into five pillars, each requiring distinct processes, tools, and people:</p>
<pre><code>┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                  THREADIT OPERATIONS                     │
├───────────┬───────────┬──────────┬──────────┬───────────┤
│  VENDOR   │  CATALOG  │  ZONE &amp;  │ CUSTOMER │  QUALITY  │
│ ONBOARDING│ MANAGEMENT│  RIDER   │ SUPPORT  │  CONTROL  │
│           │           │   OPS    │          │           │
│ Scouting  │ Photography│ Zone    │ WhatsApp │ Vendor    │
│ Pitching  │ Sizing    │ mapping  │ support  │ ratings   │
│ Setup     │ Pricing   │ Rider    │ Escala-  │ Product   │
│ Training  │ Inventory │ mgmt    │ tions    │ audits    │
│ Retention │ Updates   │ Routing  │ Feedback │ Return    │
│           │           │          │          │ analysis  │
└───────────┴───────────┴──────────┴──────────┴───────────┘
</code></pre>
<h2>Pillar 1: Vendor Onboarding</h2>
<h3>The Onboarding Playbook — Step by Step</h3>
<p>This is the most detailed operational process in the company. Every step matters because <strong>a badly onboarded vendor is worse than no vendor</strong> — they&#39;ll have a poor catalog, slow response times, and generate bad customer experiences.</p>
<h3>Step 1: Scouting (Day 1)</h3>
<p><strong>Objective:</strong> Identify and shortlist target stores in the launch zone.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Action</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Walk the zone</strong></td>
<td>Field agent physically walks through the target market area (e.g., Fancy Bazaar), identifies clothing stores, notes location, size, apparent quality</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Google Maps research</strong></td>
<td>Cross-reference with Google Maps listings for reviews, photos, hours</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Instagram/Facebook search</strong></td>
<td>Search for stores with active social media — these are Deepa-type vendors (high priority)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Create prospect list</strong></td>
<td>Spreadsheet with: Store name, location, estimated size, category (men&#39;s/women&#39;s/both), contact info, priority tier</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Target per day</strong></td>
<td>Scout 15–20 stores, shortlist 8–10 for pitching</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>Step 2: Pitching (Day 1–2)</h3>
<p><strong>Objective:</strong> Visit the store, pitch ThreadIt, get verbal commitment.</p>
<p><strong>The Pitch Script (key talking points):</strong></p>
<pre><code>1. PROBLEM: &quot;Uncle/Bhaiya, have you noticed fewer young customers 
   coming to the store compared to 5 years ago?&quot;
   [Let them talk. They&#39;ll confirm.]

2. CAUSE: &quot;They&#39;re ordering from Myntra and Amazon because it&#39;s 
   convenient. Not because those products are better than yours.&quot;
   [They&#39;ll agree passionately.]

3. SOLUTION: &quot;What if your store was on an app — like Zomato, 
   but for clothes? Customers nearby could order from you and 
   get delivery in 30 minutes.&quot;
   [Show the app / prototype.]

4. EFFORT: &quot;You don&#39;t have to do anything. Our team comes to 
   your store, photographs your products, sets up your online 
   catalog. You just approve and start receiving orders.&quot;
   [This is the key objection-killer.]

5. COST: &quot;We charge 15% commission only on successful orders. 
   No monthly fees, no upfront costs. If you don&#39;t get orders, 
   you don&#39;t pay anything.&quot;
   [Remove risk entirely.]

6. TRIAL: &quot;Your first 20 orders are at 0% commission. Try it 
   risk-free.&quot;
   [Close the deal.]
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Success criteria:</strong> Verbal commitment + exchange of phone numbers + scheduled date for catalog creation.</p>
<p><strong>Expected conversion rate:</strong> 40–50% of pitched stores agree (Assumption — based on informal conversations. Actual rate to be measured.)</p>
<h3>Step 3: Documentation &amp; Agreement (Day 2–3)</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Action</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Collect documents</strong></td>
<td>Store GST number (if registered), PAN, bank account details for payouts, Aadhaar of owner</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Digital agreement</strong></td>
<td>Simple digital agreement (DocuSign or even WhatsApp confirmation in v1) covering commission rate, payout terms, return policy, code of conduct</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>No physical contracts in v1</strong></td>
<td>Keep it lightweight. A heavy legal process scares small store owners. Digital consent via OTP verification is sufficient initially.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Store profile creation</strong></td>
<td>Enter store details in admin system: name, address, GPS coordinates, operating hours, categories, owner contact</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>Step 4: Catalog Sprint (Day 3–5)</h3>
<p>This is the most labor-intensive step. It&#39;s also the most critical.</p>
<p><strong>The Catalog Sprint Approach:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>[!IMPORTANT]<br><strong>Do NOT try to catalog the entire store.</strong> A store with 2,000 SKUs cannot be fully digitized in one visit. Instead, use the &quot;Catalog Sprint&quot; approach: <strong>photograph and list the store&#39;s top 50–100 items.</strong> These should be:</p>
<ul>
<li>Best-sellers (ask the owner: &quot;What are your top 10 products?&quot;)</li>
<li>Items with clear size availability (full size run in stock)</li>
<li>Photogenic products (they will look good in the app)</li>
<li>Seasonally relevant (what&#39;s selling right now)</li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Photography Process:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Step</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Equipment</strong></td>
<td>Smartphone (iPhone or high-end Android — camera quality matters) + portable lightbox (₹1,500–₹3,000 from Amazon) + plain white/neutral background sheet</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Photos per product</strong></td>
<td>Minimum 3: Front, Back, Close-up of fabric/detail. Flat-lay style on clean surface.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Lighting</strong></td>
<td>Natural light near a window OR portable LED light. No flash. No harsh shadows.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Naming convention</strong></td>
<td><code>[StoreName]_[Category]_[Brand]_[Color]_[Date].jpg</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Size documentation</strong></td>
<td>For each product: list ALL available sizes. Photograph the size label. Note any sizing anomalies.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Price documentation</strong></td>
<td>MRP (printed price), Store selling price (actual price after discount). Both recorded.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Time per product</strong></td>
<td>3–5 minutes (photo + data entry). 50 products = ~3–4 hours.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Photography Quality Standards:</strong></p>
<pre><code>✅ ACCEPTABLE:
- Clear, well-lit photo on white/neutral background
- Product fills 70-80% of frame
- No wrinkles (iron/steam before photographing)
- Colors appear natural (no heavy filters)
- All angles captured

❌ NOT ACCEPTABLE:
- Dark, shadowy photos
- Product on cluttered store shelf/hanger
- Blurry or out of focus
- Incorrect colors (flash making white look yellow)
- Only one angle
- Other products visible in frame
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>[!TIP]<br><strong>Invest ₹5,000–₹8,000 in a portable photography setup per field agent.</strong> The ROI is massive — good product photos directly correlate with conversion rate. A poorly photographed ₹2,000 shirt will get fewer clicks than a well-photographed ₹800 shirt. Photography quality is not optional.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Step 5: Vendor App Training (Day 5)</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Action</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Install vendor app</strong></td>
<td>On the store owner&#39;s phone (or a staff member&#39;s phone if the owner prefers)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Walk through key flows</strong></td>
<td>Receiving an order notification → Accepting/Rejecting → Marking &quot;Ready for Pickup&quot; → Handing off to rider</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Simulate a test order</strong></td>
<td>Field agent places a test order from customer app. Store owner processes it end-to-end.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Key training points</strong></td>
<td>1. Keep the app running in background (don&#39;t force-close it). 2. Respond to orders within 3 minutes. 3. Check item availability before accepting. 4. Pack in ThreadIt bag. 5. How to update inventory (&quot;out of stock&quot; toggle).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Leave behind materials</strong></td>
<td>Laminated quick-reference card with key steps + ThreadIt support WhatsApp number</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Time required</strong></td>
<td>30–45 minutes</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>Step 6: Go Live (Day 5–6)</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Action</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Final review</strong></td>
<td>Review catalog listing with vendor — are photos accurate? Prices correct? Sizes available?</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Activate store</strong></td>
<td>Mark store as &quot;Live&quot; in admin dashboard</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Monitor first 48 hours</strong></td>
<td>Field agent personally monitors the store&#39;s first 2–3 orders. Calls the vendor after each order to ensure smooth experience.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>First order guarantee</strong></td>
<td>Use targeted promotions / marketing in the zone to drive at least 1 order to the new store within 48 hours. If organic demand is low, the team can place a legitimate test order.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Onboarding Timeline Summary:</strong></p>
<pre><code>Day 1:     Scout + Pitch
Day 2-3:   Documentation + Agreement
Day 3-5:   Catalog Sprint (photography + data entry)
Day 5:     Vendor App Training
Day 5-6:   Go Live + Monitoring
Day 7-14:  Ongoing support + first order follow-up

TOTAL: 5-6 working days per store (with overlap possible)
TARGET: 1 field agent can onboard 2-3 stores per week
</code></pre>
<h2>Pillar 2: Catalog Management</h2>
<h3>The Ongoing Catalog Challenge</h3>
<p>Onboarding photography is a one-time event. But inventory changes constantly:</p>
<ul>
<li>New stock arrives weekly</li>
<li>Items get sold out</li>
<li>Prices change during sales</li>
<li>Seasonal collections rotate</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>How do we keep the catalog accurate?</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Approach</th>
<th>When to Use</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Vendor self-update</strong></td>
<td>Day-to-day</td>
<td>Vendor toggles items as &quot;in stock&quot; / &quot;out of stock&quot; in the app. Must be dead simple — one tap.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Weekly catalog refresh (call)</strong></td>
<td>Weekly</td>
<td>Field agent or support team calls vendor every Monday: &quot;Any new items? Anything out of stock that we should remove?&quot; Quick 5-minute check.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Monthly catalog sprint</strong></td>
<td>Monthly</td>
<td>Field agent visits store to photograph new arrivals, remove discontinued items, update prices. 1–2 hours per store.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>POS integration (future)</strong></td>
<td>When vendor has POS system</td>
<td>Integrate with store&#39;s billing software (Tally, Marg, EasyBill) to auto-sync inventory. Solves the accuracy problem permanently. This is a v2+ feature.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>Size Chart Standardization</h3>
<blockquote>
<p>[!CAUTION]<br><strong>Sizes in Indian retail are chaos.</strong> The same &quot;L&quot; shirt varies by 2–3 inches across brands. This is the #1 driver of size-related returns. We cannot fix this industry-wide, but we can mitigate it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>ThreadIt&#39;s Size Approach:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Strategy</th>
<th>Detail</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>ThreadIt Universal Size Chart</strong></td>
<td>Create a standardized measurement chart (chest, waist, length in inches/cm). Map every product to actual measurements, not just S/M/L labels.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Per-product measurements</strong></td>
<td>During catalog photography, field agent measures the actual garment (not the tag size). These measurements are displayed on the product page.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Size recommendation</strong></td>
<td>&quot;Based on your profile, we recommend Size M in this product&quot; — simple logic based on customer&#39;s saved measurements. Not AI in v1 — just a lookup table.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Brand-specific guidance</strong></td>
<td>Over time, accumulate data: &quot;Brand X runs small — order one size up.&quot; Show this as a tip on product pages.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Pillar 3: Zone &amp; Rider Operations</h2>
<h3>Zone Mapping</h3>
<p>ThreadIt doesn&#39;t launch city-wide. We launch <strong>zone by zone.</strong></p>
<pre><code>GUWAHATI ZONE MAP (Proposed Launch)

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                                             │
│    ZONE 1 (Launch)                          │
│    ┌───────────────────────┐                │
│    │ Fancy Bazaar          │                │
│    │ Pan Bazaar            │                │
│    │ Paltan Bazaar         │                │
│    └───────────────────────┘                │
│              │                              │
│    ZONE 2 (Week 2-3)                        │
│    ┌───────────────────────┐                │
│    │ GS Road               │                │
│    │ Ganeshguri             │                │
│    │ Zoo Road (partial)     │                │
│    └───────────────────────┘                │
│              │                              │
│    ZONE 3 (Month 2)                         │
│    ┌───────────────────────┐                │
│    │ Beltola               │                │
│    │ Six Mile              │                │
│    │ Hatigaon              │                │
│    └───────────────────────┘                │
│              │                              │
│    ZONE 4 (Month 3+)                        │
│    ┌───────────────────────┐                │
│    │ Chandmari             │                │
│    │ Dispur                │                │
│    │ Kahilipara            │                │
│    └───────────────────────┘                │
│                                             │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Zone Activation Criteria:</strong></p>
<p>A zone is only &quot;launched&quot; when these minimums are met:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Criterion</th>
<th>Minimum</th>
<th>Why</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>Active vendors in zone</td>
<td>8</td>
<td>Enough catalog variety for customers to find what they want</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Total listed products</td>
<td>200</td>
<td>Minimum browseable catalog</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Available riders (or partner coverage)</td>
<td>2 during peak hours</td>
<td>Can handle 4–6 orders/hour</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Customer marketing initiated</td>
<td>Yes</td>
<td>Demand must exist or be created before supply goes live</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>Rider Operations (v1 — Third-Party Partnership)</h3>
<p>As established in Chapter 6, we start with third-party rider partnerships rather than our own fleet.</p>
<p><strong>Partnership options in Guwahati:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Option</th>
<th>Pros</th>
<th>Cons</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Dunzo</strong></td>
<td>Established gig platform, API integration possible</td>
<td>May not have strong presence in Guwahati (needs verification)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Porter</strong></td>
<td>Strong in logistics, can handle packages</td>
<td>Primarily inter-city/heavy logistics — may not do hyperlocal</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Local bike courier services</strong></td>
<td>Cheap, know the city, flexible</td>
<td>Unreliable, no tech integration, quality variance</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Hybrid: 3–5 own riders + local partner backup</strong></td>
<td>Control core fleet, use partners for surge</td>
<td>Slightly higher fixed cost but much better quality control</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Recommendation: Hybrid model.</strong></p>
<p>Start with 3–5 dedicated riders (part-time, paid per delivery + daily minimum guarantee) for core zones during peak hours. Use a local courier partner for overflow and off-peak. This gives us quality control where it matters most (peak hours, Zone 1–2) while keeping costs variable.</p>
<p><strong>Rider Economics (v1 estimates):</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Item</th>
<th>Amount</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Per-delivery payout</strong></td>
<td>₹40–₹50</td>
<td>Based on 3–5 km average distance</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Daily minimum guarantee</strong></td>
<td>₹300/day</td>
<td>For dedicated riders who commit to peak hours (4–8 PM). If per-delivery earnings exceed this, no top-up.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Fuel allowance</strong></td>
<td>Included in per-delivery</td>
<td>Not separate</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Peak hour bonus</strong></td>
<td>+₹10/delivery</td>
<td>Evenings (5–8 PM) and weekends</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Estimated daily earnings</strong></td>
<td>₹400–₹600</td>
<td>For a rider doing 8–12 deliveries in a 6-hour shift</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>Peak Hours Analysis (Assumption — to be validated)</h3>
<pre><code>EXPECTED ORDER DISTRIBUTION (Clothing)

Morning (9 AM - 12 PM):    ░░░░░          ~15% of daily orders
                           Office-goers browsing during commute

Afternoon (12 PM - 4 PM):  ░░░░           ~10% of daily orders
                           Low — people at work/college

Evening (4 PM - 8 PM):     ░░░░░░░░░░░░░  ~45% of daily orders
                           PEAK — post-work, post-college shopping

Night (8 PM - 10 PM):      ░░░░░░░        ~20% of daily orders
                           Late browsing, tomorrow&#39;s event prep

Late Night (10 PM+):       ░░░            ~10% of daily orders
                           Impulse orders (stores may be closed)
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>[!NOTE]<br><strong>Store closing hours are a constraint.</strong> Unlike food delivery (restaurants open until 11 PM+), most clothing stores in Guwahati close by 8–9 PM. This caps our peak delivery window. Orders placed after store hours must be queued for next-day delivery — which weakens the &quot;30-minute&quot; promise. <strong>In v1, ThreadIt only operates during store hours.</strong> We clearly communicate operating hours in the app: &quot;Orders available 10 AM – 8:30 PM.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Pillar 4: Customer Support</h2>
<h3>v1 Support Strategy: WhatsApp-First</h3>
<p>We&#39;re a small team. Building an in-app support system from day one is over-engineering. Instead:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Channel</th>
<th>Usage</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>WhatsApp Business</strong></td>
<td>Primary support channel. Customer reaches out via WhatsApp. One dedicated number.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>In-app &quot;Help&quot; button</strong></td>
<td>Links to WhatsApp with pre-filled context (order ID, issue type)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Phone call</strong></td>
<td>For critical issues only (wrong delivery, rider issues). Not advertised — used by support team to call customers when needed.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Email</strong></td>
<td>Not for v1. Nobody emails for support in India for quick-commerce.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Support Escalation Matrix:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Issue</th>
<th>First Response</th>
<th>Escalation</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>&quot;Where is my order?&quot;</td>
<td>Automated: share live tracking link</td>
<td>If delayed &gt; 10 min past ETA → support agent contacts rider</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&quot;Wrong item received&quot;</td>
<td>Support agent within 15 min</td>
<td>Immediate resolution: pickup + correct item or refund</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&quot;Want to cancel order&quot;</td>
<td>Automated based on order state</td>
<td>If vendor hasn&#39;t packed: auto-cancel. If packed: support agent mediates</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&quot;Vendor didn&#39;t accept&quot;</td>
<td>Automated: reroute or refund</td>
<td>If reroute fails: support agent offers alternative + credit</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&quot;Payment issue&quot;</td>
<td>Support agent within 30 min</td>
<td>Escalate to payment gateway if needed</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&quot;Want to become a vendor&quot;</td>
<td>Collect details, schedule field visit</td>
<td>Field agent follows up within 24 hours</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Support SLAs:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Priority</th>
<th>Response Time</th>
<th>Resolution Time</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>Critical (wrong item, overcharge, rider issue)</td>
<td>&lt; 15 minutes</td>
<td>&lt; 2 hours</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>High (delayed order, partial delivery)</td>
<td>&lt; 30 minutes</td>
<td>&lt; 4 hours</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Medium (refund query, size exchange)</td>
<td>&lt; 1 hour</td>
<td>&lt; 24 hours</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Low (general inquiry, feedback)</td>
<td>&lt; 4 hours</td>
<td>&lt; 48 hours</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Pillar 5: Quality Control</h2>
<h3>Vendor Quality Scorecard</h3>
<p>Every vendor gets a quality score, updated weekly:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Weight</th>
<th>Scoring</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Order acceptance rate</strong></td>
<td>25%</td>
<td>&gt; 90% = 100 pts, 80-90% = 70 pts, &lt; 80% = 30 pts</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Fulfillment accuracy</strong> (right item, right size)</td>
<td>30%</td>
<td>&gt; 98% = 100 pts, 95-98% = 70 pts, &lt; 95% = 30 pts</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Pack time</strong> (acceptance → ready for pickup)</td>
<td>15%</td>
<td>&lt; 3 min = 100 pts, 3-5 min = 70 pts, &gt; 5 min = 30 pts</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Customer rating</strong> (1-5 stars)</td>
<td>20%</td>
<td>&gt; 4.5 = 100 pts, 4.0-4.5 = 70 pts, &lt; 4.0 = 30 pts</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Return rate</strong> (returns attributed to vendor issues)</td>
<td>10%</td>
<td>&lt; 5% = 100 pts, 5-15% = 70 pts, &gt; 15% = 30 pts</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Score-based actions:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Score</th>
<th>Status</th>
<th>Action</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>80–100</td>
<td>🟢 Excellent</td>
<td>Eligible for &quot;Top Store&quot; badge, priority listing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>60–79</td>
<td>🟡 Good</td>
<td>Normal operation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>40–59</td>
<td>🟠 Needs Improvement</td>
<td>Warning notification, field agent visit for coaching</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&lt; 40</td>
<td>🔴 At Risk</td>
<td>Listing restricted, account review, potential delisting</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Launch Team Structure</h2>
<p>For the Guwahati pilot, we need a lean team that covers all operational pillars:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Role</th>
<th>Count</th>
<th>Responsibility</th>
<th>Monthly Cost (Est.)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Founder / CEO</strong></td>
<td>1</td>
<td>Strategy, vendor pitching, investor relations, everything else</td>
<td>₹0 (equity only in early stage)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>CTO / Lead Engineer</strong></td>
<td>1</td>
<td>Build and maintain all apps, infrastructure, data</td>
<td>₹0–₹50,000 (co-founder or early equity hire)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Field Agent / Vendor Ops</strong></td>
<td>2</td>
<td>Vendor scouting, onboarding, catalog photography, vendor support</td>
<td>₹15,000–₹20,000 each</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Customer Support</strong></td>
<td>1</td>
<td>WhatsApp support, order issue resolution, customer feedback</td>
<td>₹12,000–₹15,000</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Marketing / Growth</strong></td>
<td>1</td>
<td>Social media, local marketing, customer acquisition</td>
<td>₹12,000–₹15,000 (or founder handles this initially)</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Total team: 5–6 people</strong><br><strong>Total monthly team cost: ₹54,000–₹1,20,000</strong> (Assumption — depending on co-founder arrangements and whether marketing is handled by founder)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>[!WARNING]<br><strong>This is a skeleton crew.</strong> Everyone will wear multiple hats. The founder will be pitching vendors in the morning, handling support escalations in the afternoon, and reviewing metrics at night. This is normal for pre-seed stage. The team scales only when order volume justifies it — targeting additions after 100+ orders/day.</p>
</blockquote>
