<h2>Feature Dependency Map</h2>
<p>Some features cannot be built without others. The critical path:</p>
<pre><code>FOUNDATION LAYER (Build First):
├── User auth (customer, vendor, rider, admin)
├── Store &amp; product data models
├── Location services (GPS, geocoding)
├── Payment gateway integration
│
CORE TRANSACTION LAYER (Build Second):
├── Product browsing + search ──────────────────────────┐
├── Cart + checkout ────────────────────────────────────┤
├── Order creation + state machine ─────────────────────┤── These form
├── Vendor order notification + accept/reject ──────────┤   the core
├── Rider assignment + pickup/delivery confirmation ────┤   order loop
├── Push notifications ─────────────────────────────────┘
│
EXPERIENCE LAYER (Build Third):
├── Chat (customer ↔ vendor)
├── Ratings + reviews
├── Return/exchange initiation
├── Order tracking (real-time status)
│
OPERATIONS LAYER (Build Fourth):
├── Admin dashboard (live orders + manual intervention)
├── Vendor management
├── Payout calculation
│
GROWTH LAYER (Build After Launch):
├── Live map tracking
├── Analytics
├── Promotions + coupons
├── Size recommendations
</code></pre>
<h2>Mobile Strategy</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Decision</th>
<th>Choice</th>
<th>Rationale</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Platform</strong></td>
<td>Android first, iOS later</td>
<td>85%+ of our target users (Guwahati, Tier-2 city) are on Android. iOS can wait until we enter metros.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Technology</strong></td>
<td>React Native (recommended)</td>
<td>Cross-platform — same codebase for customer, vendor, and rider apps. Faster development with a small team. Performance is sufficient for our use case.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Alternative considered</strong></td>
<td>Flutter</td>
<td>Also viable. React Native chosen because of larger developer ecosystem and easier web-app sharing (React for admin dashboard).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Web app</strong></td>
<td>Admin dashboard only (v1). Customer/vendor PWA in v2.</td>
<td>Native mobile apps are essential for push notifications and location services. Web app for admin is fine — it&#39;s used on desktop.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<blockquote>
<p>[!IMPORTANT]<br><strong>Do NOT build a customer web app or mobile website in v1.</strong> All customer traffic should go through the app. This gives us push notification capability (critical for re-engagement), location services (critical for hyperlocal), and a cleaner experience. A web fallback can come in v2 for SEO and shareable store links.</p>
</blockquote>
