<blockquote>
<p>[!TIP]<br><strong>Infrastructure cost is NOT the bottleneck.</strong> ₹15,000/month for the entire technology stack is remarkably cheap. The real costs are people (team salaries), marketing (customer acquisition), and operations (rider payouts). Don&#39;t over-invest in infrastructure before product-market fit is proven.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr>
<h1>Chapter 19 — Database Design</h1>
<h2>Core Entities</h2>
<pre><code>┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    THREADIT DATABASE SCHEMA                       │
│                                                                  │
│  ┌─────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────────┐               │
│  │  USERS  │───▶│ ADDRESSES│    │   ZONES      │               │
│  └────┬────┘    └──────────┘    └──────────────┘               │
│       │                                                          │
│       │ (role: customer/vendor/rider/admin)                      │
│       │                                                          │
│  ┌────┴────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────────┐               │
│  │ STORES  │───▶│ PRODUCTS │───▶│PRODUCT       │               │
│  └────┬────┘    └────┬─────┘    │VARIANTS      │               │
│       │              │          │(size × color) │               │
│       │              │          └──────────────┘               │
│       │              │                                          │
│  ┌────┴────┐    ┌────┴─────┐    ┌──────────────┐               │
│  │ ORDERS  │───▶│ORDER     │    │  DELIVERIES  │               │
│  └────┬────┘    │ITEMS     │    └──────────────┘               │
│       │         └──────────┘                                    │
│       │                                                          │
│  ┌────┴────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────────┐               │
│  │PAYMENTS │    │ RATINGS  │    │    CHATS     │               │
│  └─────────┘    └──────────┘    └──────────────┘               │
│                                                                  │
│  ┌──────────┐                                                   │
│  │ PAYOUTS  │  (vendor settlement records)                     │
│  └──────────┘                                                   │
│                                                                  │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</code></pre>
<h2>Schema Definition (Key Tables)</h2>
<h3>Users</h3>
<pre><code class="language-sql">CREATE TABLE users (
  id              UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  phone           VARCHAR(15) NOT NULL UNIQUE,
  name            VARCHAR(100),
  role            VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL DEFAULT &#39;customer&#39;,
                  -- &#39;customer&#39;, &#39;vendor&#39;, &#39;rider&#39;, &#39;admin&#39;
  email           VARCHAR(255),
  avatar_url      TEXT,
  is_active       BOOLEAN DEFAULT true,
  is_cod_eligible BOOLEAN DEFAULT false,
  created_at      TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW(),
  updated_at      TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()
);

-- Indexes
CREATE INDEX idx_users_phone ON users(phone);
CREATE INDEX idx_users_role ON users(role);
</code></pre>
<h3>Addresses</h3>
<pre><code class="language-sql">CREATE TABLE addresses (
  id          UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  user_id     UUID REFERENCES users(id),
  label       VARCHAR(50),  -- &#39;Home&#39;, &#39;Office&#39;, &#39;Other&#39;
  address_line TEXT NOT NULL,
  city        VARCHAR(100) DEFAULT &#39;Guwahati&#39;,
  pincode     VARCHAR(10),
  location    GEOGRAPHY(POINT, 4326) NOT NULL,  -- PostGIS
  is_default  BOOLEAN DEFAULT false,
  created_at  TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()
);

CREATE INDEX idx_addresses_user ON addresses(user_id);
CREATE INDEX idx_addresses_location ON addresses USING GIST(location);
</code></pre>
<h3>Stores</h3>
<pre><code class="language-sql">CREATE TABLE stores (
  id              UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  owner_id        UUID REFERENCES users(id),
  name            VARCHAR(200) NOT NULL,
  description     TEXT,
  banner_url      TEXT,
  phone           VARCHAR(15),
  location        GEOGRAPHY(POINT, 4326) NOT NULL,  -- PostGIS
  address         TEXT,
  zone_id         UUID REFERENCES zones(id),
  
  -- Operating hours (stored as JSON for flexibility)
  operating_hours JSONB DEFAULT &#39;{&quot;mon&quot;:{&quot;open&quot;:&quot;10:00&quot;,&quot;close&quot;:&quot;20:30&quot;}}&#39;,
  
  -- Status
  is_online       BOOLEAN DEFAULT false,
  is_active       BOOLEAN DEFAULT true,  -- admin can deactivate
  is_verified     BOOLEAN DEFAULT false,
  
  -- Business details
  gst_number      VARCHAR(20),
  bank_account    JSONB,  -- {account_no, ifsc, beneficiary_name}
  commission_rate DECIMAL(4,2) DEFAULT 15.00,
  
  -- Metrics (denormalized for fast read)
  avg_rating      DECIMAL(2,1) DEFAULT 0.0,
  total_ratings   INTEGER DEFAULT 0,
  total_orders    INTEGER DEFAULT 0,
  
  created_at      TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW(),
  updated_at      TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()
);

CREATE INDEX idx_stores_location ON stores USING GIST(location);
CREATE INDEX idx_stores_zone ON stores(zone_id);
CREATE INDEX idx_stores_active_online ON stores(is_active, is_online);
</code></pre>
<h3>Products</h3>
<pre><code class="language-sql">CREATE TABLE products (
  id            UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  store_id      UUID REFERENCES stores(id) NOT NULL,
  name          VARCHAR(300) NOT NULL,
  description   TEXT,
  category      VARCHAR(100),  -- &#39;shirts&#39;, &#39;trousers&#39;, &#39;kurtas&#39;, etc.
  subcategory   VARCHAR(100),
  brand         VARCHAR(200),
  
  -- Pricing
  mrp           DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL,
  selling_price DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL,
  
  -- Details
  fabric        VARCHAR(100),
  color         VARCHAR(100),
  pattern       VARCHAR(100),  -- &#39;solid&#39;, &#39;striped&#39;, &#39;printed&#39;, etc.
  occasion      VARCHAR(100),  -- &#39;formal&#39;, &#39;casual&#39;, &#39;ethnic&#39;, etc.
  gender        VARCHAR(20),   -- &#39;men&#39;, &#39;women&#39;, &#39;unisex&#39;, &#39;kids&#39;
  
  -- Measurements (in cm)
  measurement_chest   DECIMAL(5,1),
  measurement_waist   DECIMAL(5,1),
  measurement_length  DECIMAL(5,1),
  measurement_sleeve  DECIMAL(5,1),
  
  -- Images (array of URLs)
  images        TEXT[] NOT NULL,  -- PostgreSQL array
  
  -- Status
  is_active     BOOLEAN DEFAULT true,
  is_in_stock   BOOLEAN DEFAULT true,
  
  -- Search
  search_vector TSVECTOR,  -- Full-text search
  
  created_at    TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW(),
  updated_at    TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()
);

CREATE INDEX idx_products_store ON products(store_id);
CREATE INDEX idx_products_category ON products(category, gender);
CREATE INDEX idx_products_search ON products USING GIN(search_vector);
CREATE INDEX idx_products_active_stock ON products(is_active, is_in_stock);
</code></pre>
<h3>Product Variants (Size × Color Availability)</h3>
<pre><code class="language-sql">CREATE TABLE product_variants (
  id          UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  product_id  UUID REFERENCES products(id) NOT NULL,
  size        VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,   -- &#39;S&#39;, &#39;M&#39;, &#39;L&#39;, &#39;XL&#39;, &#39;32&#39;, &#39;34&#39;, etc.
  color       VARCHAR(50),            -- nullable if product is single-color
  sku         VARCHAR(100),           -- vendor&#39;s internal SKU if available
  is_in_stock BOOLEAN DEFAULT true,
  
  UNIQUE(product_id, size, color)
);

CREATE INDEX idx_variants_product ON product_variants(product_id);
CREATE INDEX idx_variants_stock ON product_variants(is_in_stock);
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>[!NOTE]<br><strong>Why a separate variants table?</strong> A &quot;Blue Slim Fit Shirt&quot; comes in S, M, L, XL. Each size has independent stock status. If only L is sold out, we need to mark just that variant as unavailable, not the whole product. The variants table enables per-size stock management — critical for reducing &quot;accepted then can&#39;t find&quot; failures.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Orders</h3>
<pre><code class="language-sql">CREATE TABLE orders (
  id                UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  order_number      VARCHAR(20) UNIQUE NOT NULL,  -- &#39;TH-2852&#39;
  
  -- Parties
  customer_id       UUID REFERENCES users(id) NOT NULL,
  store_id          UUID REFERENCES stores(id) NOT NULL,
  rider_id          UUID REFERENCES users(id),  -- assigned later
  
  -- Status
  status            VARCHAR(30) NOT NULL DEFAULT &#39;placed&#39;,
                    -- placed, vendor_accepted, vendor_rejected, 
                    -- packing, ready_for_pickup, rider_assigned,
                    -- picked_up, in_transit, delivered, 
                    -- completed, return_initiated, return_completed,
                    -- cancelled
  
  -- Financials
  subtotal          DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL,
  delivery_fee      DECIMAL(10,2) DEFAULT 0,
  total_amount      DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL,
  commission_amount DECIMAL(10,2),
  commission_rate   DECIMAL(4,2),
  payment_method    VARCHAR(20),  -- &#39;upi&#39;, &#39;card&#39;, &#39;cod&#39;
  payment_status    VARCHAR(20) DEFAULT &#39;pending&#39;,
                    -- pending, paid, refunded, failed
  
  -- Delivery
  delivery_address  JSONB NOT NULL,  -- snapshot of address at order time
  delivery_location GEOGRAPHY(POINT, 4326),
  estimated_delivery_minutes INTEGER,
  actual_delivery_minutes    INTEGER,
  
  -- Timestamps (for SLA tracking)
  placed_at         TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW(),
  confirmed_at      TIMESTAMPTZ,
  packed_at         TIMESTAMPTZ,
  picked_up_at      TIMESTAMPTZ,
  delivered_at      TIMESTAMPTZ,
  cancelled_at      TIMESTAMPTZ,
  
  -- Notes
  customer_note     TEXT,
  cancellation_reason TEXT,
  internal_notes    TEXT,
  
  created_at        TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW(),
  updated_at        TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()
);

CREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer ON orders(customer_id);
CREATE INDEX idx_orders_store ON orders(store_id);
CREATE INDEX idx_orders_rider ON orders(rider_id);
CREATE INDEX idx_orders_status ON orders(status);
CREATE INDEX idx_orders_date ON orders(placed_at);
</code></pre>
<h3>Order Items</h3>
<pre><code class="language-sql">CREATE TABLE order_items (
  id              UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  order_id        UUID REFERENCES orders(id) NOT NULL,
  product_id      UUID REFERENCES products(id) NOT NULL,
  variant_id      UUID REFERENCES product_variants(id),
  
  -- Snapshot at time of order (product details may change later)
  product_name    VARCHAR(300) NOT NULL,
  product_image   TEXT,
  size            VARCHAR(20),
  color           VARCHAR(50),
  quantity        INTEGER DEFAULT 1,
  unit_price      DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL,
  total_price     DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL,
  
  -- Return tracking
  return_status   VARCHAR(20),  -- null, &#39;initiated&#39;, &#39;picked_up&#39;, &#39;completed&#39;
  return_reason   VARCHAR(100),
  
  created_at      TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()
);

CREATE INDEX idx_order_items_order ON order_items(order_id);
</code></pre>
<h3>Deliveries</h3>
<pre><code class="language-sql">CREATE TABLE deliveries (
  id              UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  order_id        UUID REFERENCES orders(id) NOT NULL,
  rider_id        UUID REFERENCES users(id),
  
  -- OTPs
  pickup_otp      VARCHAR(6),
  delivery_otp    VARCHAR(6),
  
  -- Status
  status          VARCHAR(30) DEFAULT &#39;pending&#39;,
                  -- pending, rider_assigned, rider_en_route_store,
                  -- at_store, picked_up, en_route_customer,
                  -- arrived, delivered, failed
  
  -- Location tracking
  rider_location  GEOGRAPHY(POINT, 4326),
  rider_location_updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ,
  
  -- Financials
  rider_payout    DECIMAL(10,2),
  cod_amount      DECIMAL(10,2),  -- null if prepaid
  cod_collected   BOOLEAN DEFAULT false,
  
  -- Timestamps
  assigned_at     TIMESTAMPTZ,
  pickup_at       TIMESTAMPTZ,
  delivered_at    TIMESTAMPTZ,
  
  created_at      TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW(),
  updated_at      TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()
);
</code></pre>
<h3>Ratings</h3>
<pre><code class="language-sql">CREATE TABLE ratings (
  id            UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  order_id      UUID REFERENCES orders(id) NOT NULL,
  customer_id   UUID REFERENCES users(id) NOT NULL,
  store_id      UUID REFERENCES stores(id) NOT NULL,
  
  store_rating   INTEGER CHECK (store_rating BETWEEN 1 AND 5),
  product_rating INTEGER CHECK (product_rating BETWEEN 1 AND 5),
  delivery_rating INTEGER CHECK (delivery_rating BETWEEN 1 AND 5),
  review_text    TEXT,
  
  created_at     TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()
);
</code></pre>
<h3>Key Geospatial Query: Find Nearby Stores</h3>
<pre><code class="language-sql">-- Find all active, online stores within 5km of customer&#39;s location
SELECT s.*, 
       ST_Distance(s.location, ST_MakePoint(91.7362, 26.1445)::geography) 
         AS distance_meters
FROM stores s
WHERE s.is_active = true 
  AND s.is_online = true
  AND ST_DWithin(
    s.location, 
    ST_MakePoint(91.7362, 26.1445)::geography,  -- customer lng, lat
    5000  -- 5km radius in meters
  )
ORDER BY distance_meters ASC;
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>[!TIP]<br><strong>PostGIS&#39;s <code>ST_DWithin</code> with a GIST index on the geography column makes this query fast even with thousands of stores.</strong> This is why we chose PostgreSQL + PostGIS over MongoDB — geospatial queries are a first-class citizen, and we get full relational integrity for orders, payments, and user data.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Data Design Principles</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Principle</th>
<th>Implementation</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Soft deletes</strong></td>
<td>Products and stores are never hard-deleted. Set <code>is_active = false</code>. Order history must reference the product that was ordered, even if it&#39;s later removed from the catalog.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Snapshot order data</strong></td>
<td>When an order is placed, product name, price, and image are copied into <code>order_items</code>. This preserves order history even if the product is edited or deleted.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>JSONB for flexibility</strong></td>
<td>Operating hours, bank details, and delivery addresses use JSONB. Schema may change; JSONB gives flexibility without migrations.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Denormalized metrics</strong></td>
<td><code>stores.avg_rating</code> and <code>stores.total_orders</code> are denormalized for fast reads. Updated via database triggers or application logic after each order/rating.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Timestamps everywhere</strong></td>
<td>Every state transition has a timestamp. This enables SLA tracking, operational analytics, and debugging.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
