<h2>Customer Lifetime Value (LTV)</h2>
<pre><code>LTV = AOV × Orders/Month × Avg. Lifetime (months) × Contribution Margin %

Conservative: ₹1,200 × 1.0 × 6 × 10.9% = ₹785
Moderate:     ₹1,200 × 1.5 × 8 × 10.9% = ₹1,570
Optimistic:   ₹1,200 × 2.0 × 12 × 10.9% = ₹3,139
</code></pre>
<h2>LTV:CAC Ratio</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Scenario</th>
<th>LTV</th>
<th>CAC</th>
<th>LTV:CAC</th>
<th>Assessment</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>Conservative</td>
<td>₹785</td>
<td>₹120</td>
<td>6.5x</td>
<td>Good</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Moderate</td>
<td>₹1,570</td>
<td>₹100</td>
<td>15.7x</td>
<td>Excellent</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Optimistic</td>
<td>₹3,139</td>
<td>₹80</td>
<td>39.2x</td>
<td>Unlikely but aspirational</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Anything above 3x is considered healthy for a marketplace.</strong> Our conservative estimate of 6.5x is strong — IF the assumptions about repeat rate and lifetime hold.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>[!CAUTION]<br><strong>The biggest risk to LTV is repeat rate.</strong> Fashion is not food — people don&#39;t buy clothes every week. If the 30-day repeat rate is below 10%, LTV collapses and the CAC payback period extends beyond our runway. <strong>Repeat rate is the #1 metric to watch after launch.</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Monthly Fixed Cost Coverage</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Fixed Cost Category</th>
<th>Monthly Amount</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>Team (5–6 people)</td>
<td>₹80,000–₹1,20,000</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Infrastructure</td>
<td>₹10,000–₹20,000</td>
</tr>
<tr>
