<p><strong>Recommendation: Shillong as City #2.</strong> It&#39;s close to Guwahati (travel manageable), has concentrated shopping in Police Bazaar/Laitumkhrah, has active Zomato presence (proving gig economy adoption), and has a young college-going population (NEHU, St. Edmund&#39;s).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>[!WARNING]<br><strong>Do NOT jump to Tier-1 cities (Bangalore, Delhi) for City #2.</strong> The temptation will be strong — &quot;bigger market, more investor appeal.&quot; But Tier-1 cities have: (1) intense competition from Myntra/Blinkit, (2) massive geography making 30-min delivery harder, (3) higher operational costs, and (4) no founder advantage. Win the Northeast first, then go national with a proven playbook.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>New City Launch Playbook</h3>
<p>Each new city follows the same sequence:</p>
<pre><code>Week -4 to -2:  Scout → identify top 20 stores, hire 1 local field agent
Week -2 to -1:  Pitch + onboard 10-15 stores (Zone 1 only)
Week -1:        Catalog sprint, vendor training, test orders
Week 0:         Launch with targeted marketing (college campuses, social media)
Week 1-4:       Intensive ops — founder on-ground for first 2 weeks
Month 2-3:      Expand to Zone 2 if Zone 1 metrics are healthy
Month 3-6:      Hand off to local ops manager, founder returns to Guwahati
</code></pre>
<h2>Phase 3: National — Tier-2 Expansion</h2>
<p>This phase is aspirational and depends on raising funding. Target cities:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Region</th>
<th>Cities</th>
<th>Why</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><strong>Northeast</strong></td>
<td>Shillong, Jorhat, Dibrugarh, Imphal</td>
<td>Home turf, understood market</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>East</strong></td>
<td>Siliguri, Patna, Ranchi</td>
<td>Underserved by quick-commerce, strong local retail</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Central</strong></td>
<td>Bhopal, Indore, Raipur</td>
<td>Growing Tier-2 markets, young populations</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>South</strong></td>
<td>Mangalore, Mysore, Coimbatore</td>
<td>Strong retail culture, concentrated markets</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Scaling from 3 cities to 10 requires:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>₹1–3 crore in funding (seed round)</li>
<li>A city launch playbook that&#39;s repeatable without founder presence</li>
<li>A regional operations manager per 3–4 cities</li>
<li>Technology that handles multi-city operations (zone management, city-specific catalogs)</li>
</ul>
